The code statements that lead to the bug are:
```c
end_requests(fc, &fpq->processing);
fuse_abort_conn(fc, false);
```
Reason:
The bug occurs because `fuse_dev_release()` assumes it is the only function manipulating the `fpq->processing` list. However, `fuse_abort_conn()` can also access this list concurrently without any synchronization, leading to race conditions and potential data corruption when both functions attempt to modify the list simultaneously.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
```c
spin_lock(&fpq->lock);
list_splice_init(&fpq->processing, &to_end);
spin_unlock(&fpq->lock);
```
Reason:
The fix introduces a spin lock around the manipulation of the `fpq->processing` list, ensuring that only one function can access it at a time. This prevents concurrent access by `fuse_dev_release()` and `fuse_abort_conn()`, thus eliminating the race condition and ensuring data integrity when modifying the list.

